    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "The formula makes sense, but the standard deviation calculation is tricky because our lead times aren't a smooth bell curve. Sometimes a shipment just... disappears for a week or arrives in a weirdly fragmented way without any obvious reason in the data. How should I account for those random, unpredictable spikes in lead time without making our inventory levels way too high?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're correct that not all lead times fit a normal distribution. One approach to handle the variability is to use a simpler method, like the ERA (Expected Risk Approach) or the CONWIP (Conconomator Without Inventory at Pickup) method. These methods just consider the maximum lead time. For example, instead of a standard deviation, you can use the maximum lead time, resulting in a Safety Stock calculation that is simply the Maximum Lead Time x Desired Annual Tolerance for Inventory Outages x (1 - Desired Service Level).",
      "source": "target",
